# Test Scenarios

This section demonstrates my approach to identifying test scenarios from business requirements and user workflows.

## Scenario Design Approach

I identify test scenarios by considering:

- Business requirements
- User workflows
- Positive and negative paths
- Boundary conditions
- Business rules
- User roles and permissions
- Data validation
- Integration points
- Error handling
- Risk and impact

## Example Feature: Product Search

### Requirement

Users should be able to search for products using the product name or keyword.

### Test Scenarios

| ID | Test Scenario | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| TS-001 | Search using a valid product name | High |
| TS-002 | Search using a partial product name | High |
| TS-003 | Search using an invalid product name | Medium |
| TS-004 | Search with no search criteria | Medium |
| TS-005 | Search using special characters | Medium |
| TS-006 | Search using numbers | Low |
| TS-007 | Search using Arabic text | High |
| TS-008 | Search using English text | High |
| TS-009 | Search with leading/trailing spaces | Medium |
| TS-010 | Verify search results accuracy | High |
| TS-011 | Verify behavior when no results are found | High |
| TS-012 | Verify search results after changing the search keyword | Medium |

## Risk-Based Thinking

Higher priority is assigned to scenarios that can directly affect:

- Customer experience
- Revenue
- Order creation
- Product availability
- Data accuracy
- Critical business workflows

## Example Risk

**Risk:** Search returns incorrect products.

**Impact:** Customers may select the wrong product and create an incorrect order.

**Testing Focus:** Search accuracy, filtering logic, partial matches, language support, and result relevance.
